  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Above normal temperatures and dry conditions expected \nthrough midweek.\n\nA surface high pressure over the Southern US along with a zonal\nupper-level flow will allow temperatures to climb over the next\nfew days. The high slowly shifts eastward into the Atlantic by \nmidweek, causing high temperatures to rise into the 60s/70s, \nwhich is around 10-15 degrees above normal for this time of \nyear. Clear skies and dry weather are also expected each day, \nwith no rain chances through midweek. Overnight lows will be in \nthe upper 30s to low 40s each morning. Enjoy the mild \ntemperatures as a big change is in store for late week into this\nweekend as the first taste of winter arrives. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Late week system brings coldest air of the season \nalong with upslope snow potential in the mountains. \n\nA low pressure system develops across the Northern Great Plains\nmidweek and moves into the Ohio Valley on Thursday. A deep\nupper-level trough will accompany the low as it moves into the\nEastern CONUS. The system will bring a chance of rainfall to the\nMid-Atlantic, though models have been inconsistent with location\nof the low and how much moisture will be available. Best rain\nchances will be west of the Blue Ridge, with lower confidence in\nrain coverage for the Piedmont. \n\nThe low redevelops near the East Coast on Friday, but again \nmodels differ in where this occurs and slightly differ on timing\nwith some models keeping the low over the Northeastern US, \nwhile others take it out to sea quickly. The location will be \nimportant as to how much wraparound moisture is available for a \nnorthwest upslope snow event in the mountains as the upper- \nlevel trough moves over the area. Current thinking is that some \nupslope snow will be possible in the western mountains, but \nconfidence is low in how much will fall and how far south snow \ncould occur. \n\nThe bigger story for most of the area will be the colder air.\nDue to the upper-level trough, highs will be in the 40s/50s\nFriday and through the weekend. Overnight lows will not be too\nabnormal, but most of the mountains could see their first hard\nfreeze of the year, with lows in the mid 20s. The Piedmont could\nsee their first freeze of the year as well, with low 30s Sunday\nmorning. Drier and more seasonable weather returns for early \nnext week.",
